<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>7. Neural networks and deep learning on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/</link><description>Recent content in 7. Neural networks and deep learning on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Opening</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/opening/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/opening/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="opening"&gt;Opening&lt;a class="anchor" href="#opening"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-2"&gt;Today&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-2"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Your SVMs; a network with one hidden layer; many layers and backpropagation; overfitting and regularisation; convolutional networks; recurrent networks; when to use deep learning&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Reading: ISLP Chapter 10 (10.1–10.7). ESL Chapter 11&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Notebook: lecture07_neural_networks.ipynb on Google Classroom. Uses PyTorch&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;PyTorch is the most widely used deep-learning library. It is installed in Colab.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-3"&gt;From last week: your SVMs&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-3"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;SVMs bend the boundary with a kernel we choose. Today: networks that learn their own features from the raw inputs — pixels, samples of a signal&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Neural networks</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/neural-networks/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/neural-networks/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="neural-networks"&gt;Neural networks&lt;a class="anchor" href="#neural-networks"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-5"&gt;A network with one hidden layer&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-5"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/perceptron.jpg" alt="The Mark I Perceptron (1958): the first neural network, built in hardware." width="520" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;The Mark I Perceptron (1958): the first neural network, built in hardware.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: John C. Hay, Albert E. Murray, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mark_I_Perceptron,_Figure_2_of_operator%27s_manual.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, Public domain&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/diagrams/neuron.svg" alt="One neuron: weight the inputs, add them, pass the result through an activation." width="800" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;One neuron: weight the inputs, add them, pass the result through an activation.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-07/l07-a61bf43e57b3.svg" width="340" alt="Figure: A network with one hidden layer" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Diagram: inputs \(X_1, \dots, X_4\); hidden layer \(A_1, \dots, A_5\); output \(f\)&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Convolutional networks</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/convolutional-networks/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/convolutional-networks/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="convolutional-networks"&gt;Convolutional networks&lt;a class="anchor" href="#convolutional-networks"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-13"&gt;Example: thermal images of circuit boards&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-13"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/diagrams/thermal_board.svg" alt="A thermal image is a picture of temperatures: a faulty component shows as a hot spot." width="760" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;A thermal image is a picture of temperatures: a faulty component shows as a hot spot.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-07/l07-5226a94f99df.svg" width="340" alt="Figure: Example: thermal images of circuit boards" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-07/l07-0f43a9cc3f98.svg" width="340" alt="Figure: Example: thermal images of circuit boards" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Images: normal board and faulty board, \(24 \times 24\) pixels, \(^\circ\)C&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;Infrared (thermal) cameras are used for inspecting electronics: a failing component or a bad solder joint runs hot.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Recurrent networks</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/recurrent-networks/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/recurrent-networks/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="recurrent-networks"&gt;Recurrent networks&lt;a class="anchor" href="#recurrent-networks"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-18"&gt;Sequences: recurrent networks&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-18"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="diagram"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/diagrams/rnn_unrolled.svg" alt="A recurrent network reads a sequence one step at a time, carrying a memory forward." width="760" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;A recurrent network reads a sequence one step at a time, carrying a memory forward.&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-07/l07-51f16136f137.svg" width="340" alt="Figure: Sequences: recurrent networks" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Diagram: inputs \(x_{t-3}, \dots, x_t\); hidden states connected in a chain; forecast from the last&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A recurrent network reads a sequence one step at a time. Its hidden state \(h\) carries a memory of what came before, updated at every step with the same weights&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>When to use deep learning</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/when-to-use-deep-learning/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-07/when-to-use-deep-learning/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="when-to-use-deep-learning"&gt;When to use deep learning&lt;a class="anchor" href="#when-to-use-deep-learning"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-21"&gt;When to use deep learning&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-21"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/gpu.jpg" alt="Deep learning needs graphics cards (GPUs) and a lot of data." width="460" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;Deep learning needs graphics cards (GPUs) and a lot of data.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Timothy Borkowski, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Graphics_Card_Diamond_Viper_330_with_Nvidia_%28RIVA_128%29_GPU.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY-SA 3.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Deep learning shines: images, sound, vibration signals, text; raw inputs where you do not know the right features; large datasets&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Try simpler models first: a table of measured features (our machines: the forest won); small datasets; when you must explain the model&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>